Thank You Stefan and thank you to the  audience for being here and thank you to  the organizers and thank you to the  staff I'm sure who has been working  really hard to make this happen so  welcome to the final session of the  symposium invisible devoted to projects  that explore the connections between  perspectives human or mechanical  perspectives and the various things that  fly under the radar both because of that  very perspectives limitations on the one  hand and despite its very best efforts  on the other in order to give you a  handy and accessible way to process the  panel you're all about to experience  I want to refashion a popular phrase  that I'm very certain we all know the  phrase is beauty is in the eye of the  beholder and its newly refashioned  version for the sole purpose of making  this panel cohere for you in very brief  form is now the following presto  change-o it's this the visible is in the  eye of the beholder in other words what  the beholder can see is contingent it is  neither absolute nor universal what  counts as worthy of being seen what  counts as necessary to see and where a  seeable thing falls on one's priority  list of things to see our consequence of  how seeing is wielded and who or what is  doing the seeing in the first place  esteemed art critic Jonathan Crary  supplies the basis for this theory when  he reminds us that seeing is susceptible  to the wildly unpredictable foibles of  the human body which houses the  otherwise respectable activity of seeing  and this is what he says from the  beginning of the 19th century a science  of vision will tend to mean in  an interrogation of the physiological  makeup of the human subject rather than  the mechanics of light and optical  transmission it is a moment when the  visible escapes from the timeless order  of the camera obscura and becomes lodged  within the unstable physiology and  temporality of the human body the real  importance of physiology is that it  became the arena for new types of FSM  all at the epistemological  reflection that depended on knowledge  about the eye and processes of vision it  signals how the body was becoming the  sight of both power and truth end quote  and when bodies are socially modified I  like this word socially modified that I  came up with and when bodies are  socially modified with a racial economic  national ethnic sexual or gender  identity  seeing becomes personal and political  indeed customized for the particular  body that the eye is lodged in  literature professor that I am it would  be remiss of me not to offer you a  literary example of what this all means  a literary example of what the  implications are but I won't go the  obvious route via Ralph Ellison's 1952  novel Invisible Man about a black male  protagonist rendered in many ways  invisible by racism instead I'll read  you a brief passage from Toni Morrison's  first novel published in 1970 titled The  Bluest Eye about a young black girl  who's suffering a similar fate firmly  wishes she had blue eyes so that those  who beheld her could discern not only  beauty but also the simple fact that she  was visible in the first place in this  scene that I will read to you  she's at a small shop and about to buy  some candy she pulls off her shoe and  takes out three pennies the gray-head of  mr. yak about ski looms over the counter  he urges his eyes out of his thoughts to  encounter her his eyes are blue eyes  bleed dropped slowly like Indian summer  moving imperceptibly toward fall  he looks toward her somewhere between  retina and object between vision and  view his eyes draw back hesitate and  hover at some fixed point in time and  space he senses that he need not waste  the effort of a glance he does not see  her because for him there is nothing to  see how can a 52 year old white  immigrant storekeeper see a little black  girl nothing in his life even suggested  the feat was possible not to say  desirable or necessary unquote so query  the art critic and Morrison the novelist  encouraged us to ask how does an  investment in yourself as better than  thou deny your eyesight and your  holistic self their ability to realize  their fullest potential how have we been  socialized only to see certain people  certain contingencies or a certain way  of proceeding in life what exactly do we  stand to lose in relegating people  places and things - invisible non  factors indeed - the immaterial in  multiple meanings of that term so while  invisible considers the benefits and  costs associated with the inherent  limitations of our eyes as well as the  limitations we learn to place on them  ourselves it is my hope that the theme  of the invisible will open up the  opportunity  for a scintillating conversation about  the marvels of technology the feats of  the imagination when it compensates for  what we cannot see that would like to  see reified in our lifetimes something  like the imagination as prosthetic eyes  if you will and finally about the  sinister uses of invisible things  created for the express purpose of  blindsiding us in the here and now  making a tomorrow impossible for our  imaginations to even project themselves  into and on that invisibly hopeful note  the presentations will follow the order  listed in the program we'll have  questions at the completion of the three  presentations which begin with Professor  George Barba spaciest thank  [Applause]
 

